# CORS

Browsers do not let JavaScript on one site read responses from another. A
page on `https://app.example.com` that calls `fetch` on
`https://api.example.com` is blocked, unless the API's responses say that
site is allowed. The rules for saying so are called cross-origin resource
sharing, or CORS.

You need this page if a browser calls your Howdy app from pages served
somewhere else: a separate frontend, a single-page app on its own domain, or
a dev server on another port. You do not need it when Howdy serves the pages
too, or when the callers are not browsers. CORS is enforced by browsers
only; `curl` and other servers ignore it.

## A policy

Build a policy, turn it into middleware, and add it to the app:

```gleam
import gleam/http
import howdy
import howdy/cors

pub fn app() {
  let policy =
    cors.new()
    |> cors.allow_origins(["https://app.example.com"])
    |> cors.allow_methods([http.Get, http.Post, http.Delete])
    |> cors.allow_headers(["content-type", "authorization"])
    |> cors.max_age(600)

  howdy.new()
  |> howdy.middleware(cors.middleware(policy))
  |> howdy.controller(user_controller())
}
```

For a public API with no cookies or sign-in, `cors.allow_all()` is a
complete policy: any origin, the default methods, and whatever request
headers the browser asks for.

```gleam
howdy.new()
|> howdy.middleware(cors.middleware(cors.allow_all()))
```

## Origins

An origin is the scheme, host and port a page was loaded from, such as
`https://app.example.com` or `http://localhost:5173`. `cors.new()` allows
none, so a policy must say which to allow:

- `allow_origins` takes an exact list. Give each origin in full, with no
  path and no trailing slash. Comparison ignores case. Anything that is not
  an origin panics when the policy is built, so a typo shows up at startup.
- `allow_any_origin` allows every site, and answers with
  `access-control-allow-origin: *`.
- `allow_origins_matching` takes a function from the origin to a `Bool`, for
  rules a list cannot express, such as every subdomain of your own domain:

```gleam
import gleam/string

cors.new()
|> cors.allow_origins_matching(fn(origin) {
  string.ends_with(origin, ".example.com")
})
```

The function receives the `origin` header exactly as the browser sent it,
and runs on every cross-origin request, so keep it quick. Be careful with
matching: a test for `string.contains(origin, "example.com")` would also
allow `https://example.com.evil.net`.

## The rest of the policy

- `allow_methods` lists the methods a cross-origin request may use. The
  default is `GET`, `HEAD`, `POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH` and `DELETE`.
- `allow_headers` lists the request headers a page may send beyond the few
  browsers always allow. You need `content-type` for JSON bodies and
  `authorization` for bearer tokens. `allow_any_header` allows whatever the
  browser asks for.
- `expose_headers` lists the response headers JavaScript may read. Without
  it a script sees only a few basic ones such as `content-type`, so a page
  that reads `x-ratelimit-remaining` needs it exposed.
- `max_age` is how many seconds a browser may remember a preflight answer,
  which saves it asking again before every request.
- `allow_credentials` lets cross-origin requests carry cookies and
  `authorization` headers.

### Credentials

By default a browser makes cross-origin requests without cookies. If your
frontend signs in with a cookie set by the API, the policy needs
`cors.allow_credentials()`, and the page must ask for them too, with
`fetch(url, { credentials: "include" })`.

Credentials cannot be combined with `allow_any_origin`. That pairing would
let any website on the internet make signed-in requests as your visitors,
so `cors.middleware` panics at startup if a policy has both. List the
origins instead.

## What the middleware does

Requests with no `origin` header are not cross-origin, and pass straight
through.

Before a cross-origin request that could change something, a browser first
sends a *preflight*: an `OPTIONS` request asking whether the real one is
allowed. The middleware answers it directly with `204`, and the handler
never runs:

```
access-control-allow-origin: https://app.example.com
access-control-allow-methods: GET, POST, DELETE
access-control-allow-headers: content-type, authorization
access-control-max-age: 600
vary: origin
```

If the origin is not allowed, the `204` carries none of those headers, and
the browser blocks the real request. You do not need to declare `OPTIONS`
routes for this: the [router](/docs/controllers/router) answers `OPTIONS`
for any path that has routes, through the controller's middleware.

Any other request with an `origin` runs as normal, and the response gains
`access-control-allow-origin` when the origin is allowed. That includes
error responses, so a page can read the body of a `422` from your API
rather than seeing an unexplained CORS failure.

Responses carry `vary: origin`, which tells caches that the answer depends
on who asked.

## Where to add it

Add CORS before any [middleware](/docs/middleware/overview) that can reject
a request, such as authentication or rate limiting:

```gleam
howdy.new()
|> howdy.middleware(logger.log)
|> howdy.middleware(cors.middleware(policy))
|> howdy.middleware(require_api_key)
```

Browsers send preflights without credentials. If `require_api_key` ran
first, it would answer every preflight with `401`, and the browser would
never send the real request.

Two limits to know about:

- A request to a path with no routes is a `404` before any middleware runs,
  so it has no CORS headers. In the browser that shows up as a CORS error,
  not a `404`. If a page reports one, check the URL first.
- CORS is not access control. It tells browsers which pages may read your
  responses; it does not stop anyone making requests. Protect routes with a
  [guard](/docs/controllers/guards).
- CORS does not stop cross-site form posts either. A browser sends those
  without a preflight, cookies attached. If visitors sign in with a cookie,
  add [CSRF protection](/docs/middleware/csrf).

## Testing CORS

Send the headers a browser would. A preflight is an `OPTIONS` request with
an `origin` and an `access-control-request-method`:

```gleam
import gleam/http
import gleam/http/response
import howdy/testing

pub fn preflight_is_allowed_test() {
  let res =
    testing.request(http.Options, "/users")
    |> testing.header("origin", "https://app.example.com")
    |> testing.header("access-control-request-method", "POST")
    |> testing.send(app())

  assert res.status == 204
  assert response.get_header(res, "access-control-allow-origin")
    == Ok("https://app.example.com")
}

pub fn other_sites_are_not_allowed_test() {
  let res =
    testing.get("/users")
    |> testing.header("origin", "https://evil.example")
    |> testing.send(app())

  assert response.get_header(res, "access-control-allow-origin") == Error(Nil)
}
```
